        <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Last authors:&lt;/b&gt; Lishu Wu&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'&gt;Score 7/10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;b&gt;B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why:&lt;/b&gt; Directly related to monolayer 2D superconductors and van der Waals heterostructures.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tags:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#material&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#superconductivity&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#2D-materials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03462-6/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3462_Figa_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Abstract:&lt;/b&gt; Monolayer superconductors enter the circuit&lt;/p&gt;</description></item>
    
        <item rdf:about="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03473-3">
            <title>[7] Reproducibility by design</title>
            <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03473-3</link>
            <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><b>Authors:</b> Authors not available in source RSS</p><p><span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'>Score 7/10</span> <b>B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT</b></p><p><b>Why:</b> Addresses experimental design and material growth in moiré and twisted bilayer systems.</p><p><b>Tags:</b> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#method</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#moire</span> <span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'>#2D-materials</span></p><p><img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03473-3/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3473_Figa_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /></p><hr/><p><b>Abstract:</b> The sensitivity of moiré materials to crystal quality and twist angle can make experimental results difficult to replicate. Reproducibility should therefore be built into material growth, device fabrication and experimental design from the outset.</p>]]></content:encoded>
            <dc:title>[7] Reproducibility by design</dc:title>
            
            <dc:identifier>doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03473-3</dc:identifier>
            <dc:source>Nature Physics, Published online: 2026-09-09; | doi:10.1038/s41567-026-03473-3</dc:source>
            <dc:date>2026-09-09</dc:date>
            <prism:publicationName>Nature Physics</prism:publicationName>
            <prism:doi>10.1038/s41567-026-03473-3</prism:doi>
            <prism:url>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-026-03473-3</prism:url>
        <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Authors:&lt;/b&gt; Authors not available in source RSS&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style='display:inline-block;padding:4px 9px;border-radius:999px;background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b;font-weight:700;'&gt;Score 7/10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;b&gt;B_IMPORTANT_CONDMAT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why:&lt;/b&gt; Addresses experimental design and material growth in moiré and twisted bilayer systems.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tags:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#moire&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style='display:inline-block;margin:2px 4px 2px 0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:999px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3;font-size:12px;'&gt;#2D-materials&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41567-026-03473-3/MediaObjects/41567_2026_3473_Figa_HTML.png' style='max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;' /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Abstract:&lt;/b&gt; The sensitivity of moiré materials to crystal quality and twist angle can make experimental results difficult to replicate. Reproducibility should therefore be built into material growth, device fabrication and experimental design from the outset.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item>
    
